end additional random features to the vertex features in GNN. As the green curve with tag \u201cFoldable + Rand Feat.\u201d shown, GNN can perfectly fit the foldable data D2, which validates Theorem 5.1 and the first conclusion in Theorem 5.2.\nOptimal value and solution Then we take the feasible instances from sets D1 and D2 and form new datasets Dfeasible1 and Dfeasible2 , respectively. On Dfeasible1 and Dfeasible2 , we validate that GNN is able to approximate the optimal objective value and one optimal solution. Figure 4a shows that, by restricting datasets to unfoldable instances, or by appending random features to the graph, one can train a GNN that has arbitrarily small approximation error for the optimal objective value. Such conclusions validates Theorems 4.3 and 5.2. Figure 4b shows that GNNs can even approximate an optimal solution of MILP, though it requires a much larger size than the case of approximating optimal objective. Theorems 4.4 and 5.3 are validated."
